> Companies that do not decarbonize may face increasing transition risks. Global climate commitments, regulations, and incentive schemes have significantly accelerated in the last decade, particularly since the Paris Agreement was adopted in two thousand fifteen. While the world is far away from achieving the one point five degrees Celsius ambition, significant progress has been made across the world, albeit at different speeds. The following actions are the most notable:
> 
> Over one hundred forty countries, including China, the European Union, India, and the United States, covering eighty-eight percent of global emissions, have made national net-zero commitments. At COP thirty in Brazil, many countries are expected to strengthen their commitments further.
> 
> In the European Union Green Deal, Europe has followed up its net-zero commitment with the most ambitious emission reduction legislation globally, including initiatives such as tightening the emissions cap of its Emissions Trading System, introducing an emission trading scheme for non-ETS sectors, banning new internal combustion engine car sales by two thousand thirty-five, and enacting rules to drive the adoption of sustainable fuels and hydrogen.
> 
> The United States introduced its Inflation Reduction Act in two thousand twenty-two, which drives billions of dollars of investments in green technologies such as electric vehicles, renewables, hydrogen, and carbon capture, utilization, and storage.
> 
> Accelerating climate action creates transition risks for companies. The Task Force on Climate-Related Financial Disclosures identified four main types of transition risks:
> 
> - Policy and legal, such as carbon pricing rules and the risk of litigation.
> - Technological, such as lower-carbon ways to make steel or power large ships that disrupt incumbents.
> - Market, meaning shifts in supply and demand for commodities, products, and services.
> - Reputation, stemming from negative stakeholder perceptions of a company’s climate actions.
> 
> Similar to physical risks, transition risks can materialize through additional financial costs. They are equally difficult to predict because they depend on future government decisions, future technological innovation, and other unknowns.
